  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 314 AM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nLight rain associated with a passing cold front continues to make \nits way slowly eastward early this morning. Its expected that this \nline should clear out of western Wisconsin around sunrise. The upper-\nlevel low that is parent to this cold front will sweep across the \nnorthern half of MN into the Great Lakes region today. Wrap-around \nprecipitation is expected to move southeastward later this morning \ninto the afternoon mainly over northern Minnesota. One thing to make \nnote of is latest NBM guidance wanted to sprinkle a few isolated \nareas of snow shower chances across central MN this afternoon. \nHowever, am reluctant to include any snow mentions locally \ngiven lack of forcing in the DGZ and available moisture \nportrayed in latest forecast soundings. Winds will be gusty \nonce again between 20-30 mph as northwest flow will be driving \nin cooler temperatures. Highs today will reach the upper 30s to \nlower 40s. For this evening into tonight, very weak ascent \ncombined with some moisture in the low- levels could help \nproduce some pre- dawn few flurries over portions of Rusk and \nChippewa Counties in western WI. \n\nActivity virtually becomes dormant after today's system clears. A \n1030mb high will become the dominant feature for Monday and Tuesday. \nForecast high temperatures each day will range in the upper 20s to \nmid 30s. Monday night still appears to be the coldest period as lows \nuniformly dip down into the mid teens. As high pressure departs \nTuesday night, another weak wave moves across the northern plains \nhowever appears to be moisture starved at this time. It is not until \nthe this wave advances further southeast later this week and becomes \norganized over the southern Great Lakes Region. Temperatures for the \nsecond half of the week return back to near 40 degrees with lows in \nthe 20s.\n\nA couple of fun climate facts for the today (and yesterday), \nOur climate site at EAU measured 1.04\" of daily rainfall of \n11/21. This amount surpasses the previous record amount of \n0.81\" set back in 1898. Now in terms of snowfall. traditionally \nat the end of November we average just under 7 seven inches of \nsnow at MSP. Interestingly if we manage to escape any measurable\nsnow over the next ten days, we will pull of a feat we haven't \ndone since 1880 (i.e. 0.0\" measured monthly snowfall for Nov). \nOn the flip side if we do squeeze out only a trace of snow \nbefore the month ends, then we will join past Novembers of 2009,\n1963, 1939, and 1928.",
